import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var o={energyConservation:{theory:{concept:"[[The law of conservation of energy: in an isolated system the total energy stays constant \u2014 energy is neither created nor destroyed, it only changes from one form to another or passes from one body to another.]]"},section:{forms:{title:"Two forms of mechanical energy",text:"A body high up has potential energy E\u209A = m\xB7g\xB7h: the higher and heavier, the more. A moving body has kinetic energy E\u2096 = \xBD\xB7m\xB7v\xB2: twice as fast means four times as much. Both are measured in joules (J). When the block slides down, h falls and v grows: potential energy turns into kinetic energy. When it climbs the other ramp, the opposite happens."},conservation:{title:"The total stays the same",text:"[[Without friction E\u2096 + E\u209A stays equal to m\xB7g\xB7H, the potential energy at the start.]] Watch the energy bar: its colours change, but its height never does. That is why a block released from 4 m climbs back to exactly 4 m on the other side."},friction:{title:"Friction turns energy into heat",text:"With friction, the block does not climb back as high. [[The 'missing' energy is not lost: friction has turned it into heat Q, which warms the block and the track a little.]] Then E\u2096 + E\u209A + Q = m\xB7g\xB7H still holds exactly. On each pass more energy turns into heat, until the block stops for good at the bottom."},path:{title:"Only the height matters",text:"Without friction, the speed at the bottom depends only on the starting height: v = \u221A(2\xB7g\xB7H). A steep or a gentle ramp, a heavy or a light block \u2014 the speed at the bottom is the same, and the block climbs to the same height on a ramp of any angle. Only the time it takes changes. Friction breaks this rule: on a longer path friction works longer, and more energy turns into heat."},joule:{title:"Joule's experiment",text:"In the 1840s James Prescott Joule let a falling weight turn a paddle wheel inside a tank of water. The paddles stirred the water and it became slightly warmer. Joule measured that the same amount of mechanical work always gave the same amount of heat \u2014 showing that heat is a form of energy. The unit of energy, the joule, is named after him: 1 J is the work done by a force of 1 N over 1 m."},everyday:{title:"Energy changes all around us",text:"A roller coaster is pulled up once and then runs by itself, trading height for speed \u2014 and every hill must be lower than the first because of friction. A cyclist free-wheels down a hill and up the next one. When a car brakes, its kinetic energy becomes heat in the brakes. Hydroelectric dams turn the potential energy of water into electricity."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A 2 kg block starts at 4 m. What is its energy? (About 78 J.) Without friction, how fast is it at the bottom? (About 8.9 m/s.) How high does it climb on a steeper ramp? (The same 4 m.) With friction it climbs only 3 m. Where did the energy of 1 m go? (It became heat, about 20 J.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{kinetic:"Kinetic energy: the energy of motion",potential:"Potential energy: the energy of height",conservation:"Conservation of energy: the total equals the starting energy",speed:"Speed at the bottom without friction",heat:"Heat made by friction along a path of length s",normal:"Normal force on a slope of angle \u03B8"},variable:{mass:"Mass",speed:"Speed",height:"Height above the bottom",startHeight:"Starting height",heat:"Heat made by friction",mu:"Friction coefficient",path:"Distance slid"},assumption:{point:"The block is treated as a point; it does not tip, roll or leave the track, and air resistance is ignored.",corners:"The corners between the ramps and the floor are perfectly smooth, so no energy is lost there.",friction:"One friction coefficient is used both for sliding and for holding the block still.",scale:"The block and the arrow are drawn larger than life. The energy bar always has the same height: the starting energy."},param:{height:"Starting height",friction:"Friction coefficient \u03BC",mass:"Mass of the block",rightAngle:"Angle of the right ramp",leftAngle:"Angle of the left ramp",gravity:"Gravity",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{height:"The block starts at rest at this height.",friction:"0 is a perfectly slippery track. Friction turns energy into heat.",mass:"Does a heavier block go faster at the bottom? Try it.",rightAngle:"Without friction the block reaches the same height on any ramp."},preset:{noFriction:"No friction",ice:"Ice",rough:"Rough"},measure:{heat:"Heat from friction",total:"Total energy",height:"Height",rightPeak:"Highest point on the right ramp",mechanical:"Mechanical energy",bottomSpeed:"Speed at the bottom without friction",heatShare:"Share of the energy turned into heat"},view:{kinetic:"E\u2096 {value}",potential:"E\u209A {value}",heat:"Q (heat) {value}",total:"Total {value}",start:"Start: {height}",peak:"Highest: {height}",barsOption:"Energy bar",velocityOption:"Velocity"},graph:{energy:"Kinetic, potential and heat energy",height:"Height of the block"},challenge:{tenAtBottom:{title:"10 m/s at the bottom",description:"On the Earth and without friction, start the block high enough to reach 10 m/s at the bottom.",hint:"v = \u221A(2gH), so H = v\xB2 / (2g) \u2248 5.1 m."},sameHeight:{title:"The same height on a different ramp",description:"Without friction, make the two ramps differ by at least 10\xB0 and show that the block still climbs back to its starting height.",hint:"Change the angle of the right ramp and run the simulation."},halfWay:{title:"Only half way up",description:"With friction, make the block climb the right ramp to about half of its starting height (45\u201355 %).",hint:"More friction turns more energy into heat. Try values between 0.1 and 0.2."},allHeat:{title:"All energy to heat",description:"Make the block stop for good at the bottom, so that all its energy has become heat.",hint:"With enough friction it stops on the flat floor, where its potential energy is zero."},heavySpeed:{title:"Heavy on the Moon",description:"On the Moon and without friction, start a block of at least 15 kg so that it reaches 4 m/s at the bottom.",hint:"The mass does not matter: v = \u221A(2gH) with g = 1.62 m/s\xB2."}}}},a={sim:o};export{a as default,o as sim};
